## 7. False Positive Reduction Strategies

### Patterns observed across Atlassian implementations

| Strategy | Implementation |
|----------|---------------|
| **Minimum cost floor** | Don't flag services spending <$500/week (noise) |
| **Multi-signal consensus** | All checks must agree on anomaly direction (spike OR drop) |
| **Planned event whitelist** | Filter services with known seasonality/events |
| **Service whitelisting** | Exclude services known to be inherently spiky |
| **Tiered thresholds** | Higher-cost services get tighter % thresholds but lower absolute bars |
| **Cold start suppression** | Don't alert until minimum history exists (6-10 weeks) |
| **Non-zero filtering** | Exclude idle/zero-cost periods from baseline |
| **Context enrichment** | Correlate with deploys, incidents, migrations before alerting |

### Performance Benchmarks (from Atlassian's Model Assessment)

**Original model** (Prophet + rolling avg + ranking + % change):
| Metric | Combined | High Magnitude | Low Magnitude |
|--------|----------|----------------|---------------|
| F1 Score | 0.558 | 0.364 | 0.625 |
| Recall | 0.632 | 0.286 | 0.833 |
| Precision | 0.500 | 0.500 | 0.500 |

Key insight: The model performs better on low-magnitude anomalies (F1=0.625) than high-magnitude ones (F1=0.364), suggesting that large spikes often have legitimate explanations (planned growth) that the model doesn't account for.

**Source**: [Anomaly Detection Model Assessment](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CEDS/pages/3331883290)

