## Finding 5: Platform Guardrails and Gate Definitions

### What it is
Launchpad enforces platform guardrails to manage resources, costs, and lifecycle of ephemeral test deployments. These are the "gates" that prevent abuse and ensure safe operation.

### How it works

**Compute Guardrails:**

| Guardrail | Enforced By | Details |
|-----------|------------|---------|
| No auto-scaling (1 node only) | TEAMServe | Single replica in chart templates |
| TTL node cleanup | Launchpad | Default 2hr; cron job shuts down expired endpoints |
| Limit node types | Launchpad +TS | Allowlist: `g5.2xlarge`, `g6e.xlarge` (approved GPU types) |
| User limits | Launchpad | Restricted on ML Studio use-case ID or tarot component ref |
| Budget cap (total nodes) | TBD | Maximum concurrent nodes for launchpad workloads |
| Cost attribution | Launchpad + TS | Pod tagging for per-user/per-team cost allocation |
| Authenticated requests | Launchpad | Users go via SDK → intermediate service → Slauth check |

**V1Scope Platform Controls:**
- Enforced TTL (default 2 hours, max configurable)
- Restricted replicas (single node only)
- Approved instance types: `t3.x` (default CPU), `g5.xlarge`, `g6e.xlarge`
- Approved containers (Triton versions)
- Machine allocation limits
- Scheduled sweeps to remove unused endpoints
- No auto-scaling

**Deployment Strategies:**
- Strategy: **Recreate** (not RollingUpdate) — because single GPU per pod
- Grace period: 30 seconds for termination
- GPU reuse: same instance, no new billing on update

### How it applies
The skill/tool must validate inputs against these guardrails before submitting:
1. Check `instance_type` against allowed list
2. Ensure TTL is within allowed bounds
3. Validate container is approved
4. Check user hasn't exceeded concurrent deployment limits

### Source
- [RFC: Launchpad Upgraded – Kubernetes Deploy](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AO2/pages/5852226559)
- [TS / Launchpad API Contract](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5834564444)

