## Executive Summary

Launchpad is Atlassian's internal ML Platform pre-deployment service that enables ML engineers and data scientists to deploy AI models on ephemeral GPU infrastructure for testing and validation before production rollout. It operates as an intermediary between ML Registry (Tarot V3), Deployment Store, and TEAMServe (the production-grade model serving platform). The system has evolved from a SageMaker-only backend (v1/v2) to supporting TEAMServe Kubernetes deployments (v3), with both deployment backends currently available.

---

## Finding 1: TEAMServe LaunchPad API — Core REST Endpoints

### What it is
The TEAMServe LaunchPad API (`teamserve-api`) is a RESTful service that manages the full lifecycle of ephemeral model deployments on Kubernetes GPU infrastructure via ArgoCD.

### How it works

**Endpoint Overview:**

| Method | Endpoint | Purpose |
|--------|----------|---------|
| POST | `/api/v1/model-deployment/launch` | Create deployment |
| PUT | `/api/v1/model-deployment/update` | Update model version (hot-swap) |
| GET | `/api/v1/model-deployment/status/{id}` | Get live status |
| GET | `/api/v1/model-deployment/list` | List deployments |
| POST | `/api/v1/model-deployment/destroy` | Delete deployment |
| POST | `/api/v1/model-deployment/set-expiry/{id}` | Extend TTL |

**POST `/launch` — Create Deployment Request:**
```json
{
  "deployment_id": "dev-test-001",
  "component_ref": "ml.registry.model.component",
  "version_ref": "v1.0.0",
  "resource_id": "resource-uuid",
  "environment": "dev",
  "container": "teamserve/nvidia/tritonserver:25.09-py3",
  "instance_type": "g6e.xlarge",
  "expiry_time_epoch": 1730318400,
  "user_id": "john.doe@atlassian.com",
  "container_args": {
    "loadModel": "my_model"
  }
}
```

**Required Fields:**
- `deployment_id` — Unique identifier (lowercase alphanumeric + hyphens)
- `component_ref`, `version_ref`, `resource_id` — Deployment store references (from Tarot V3 ML Registry)
- `environment` — Target environment (dev/staging/prod)
- `container_args.loadModel` — Model name to load in Triton

**Optional Fields:**
- `container` — Triton version (default: `25.09-py3`)
- `instance_type` — GPU type (default: `g6e.xlarge`)
- `expiry_time_epoch` — Cleanup time (default: now + 1 hour)
- `user_id`, `team_name` — Cost attribution

**Response:**
```json
{
  "deployment_id": "dev-test-001",
  "instance_type": "g6e.xlarge",
  "expiry_time_epoch": 1730318400
}
```

**PUT `/update` — Hot-Swap Model:**
```json
{
  "deployment_id": "dev-test-001",
  "component_ref": "ml.registry.model.component",
  "version_ref": "v1.0.1",
  "expiry_time_epoch": 1730322000
}
```
- Updates model artifacts (new version downloaded)
- Pod restarts on same GPU — no new billing
- TTL extended

### How it applies
A tool wrapping these APIs would expose operations: `launch_deployment`, `get_deployment_status`, `update_deployment`, `destroy_deployment`, `extend_ttl`. Each maps1:1 to the REST endpoints. The AI agent needs to understand that these are ephemeral test deployments, not production endpoints.

