## Stream 2: Symphony Orchestration and3-Tier Trust Framework

### 2.1 What It Is

Atlassian Symphony is the **continuous AI employee orchestration platform** — the fleet manager that watches work systems (Jira, JSM, ML pipelines), spawns agents per work item, supervises execution, and enforces the AI SDLC policies.

**Source**: [Atlassian Symphony: AI Employee Orchestration Platform](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6933824809)

### 2.2 The Orchestration Loop

```
Watch → Bootstrap (ORBIT context) → Execute (Agent Runtime) → Supervise → Close → Learn
```

| Phase | What Happens | ORBIT Role | Policy Role |
|-------|-------------|------------|-------------|
| **Watch** | Monitors control planes (Jira, JSM, ML queue) for new work items | — | — |
| **Bootstrap** | Spawns agent, loads scoped context pack | **READ**: Context pack loaded for service/repo/model | — |
| **Execute** | Agent takes actions within policy scope | Context used for grounding decisions | 🟢/🟡/🔴 enforcement |
| **Supervise** | Monitors health; restarts crashed agents | — | Escalation on policy boundary hits |
| **Close** | Delivers output; captures reasoning trace | — | Human reviews outcome |
| **Learn** | Completed work enriches context layer | **WRITE**: Activity signals enrich future packs | Policy gaps identified, updates proposed |

### 2.3 The 3-Tier Autonomy Model (Symphony's Runtime Enforcement)

| Tier | Signal | Meaning | Trigger |
|------|--------|---------|---------|
| **1— Autonomous** | 🟢 | Agent acts without human approval | Known pattern, within policy scope, bounded blast radius |
| **2 — Confirm** | 🟡 | Agent proposes action with reasoning; human approves with single click | Novel patterns or higher-stakes decisions |
| **3 — Escalate** | 🔴 | Agent hits hard policy boundary; pages human with full context | Hard boundaries that always require human decision |

**Key properties**:
- Policies are **version-controlled** (in git alongside code)
- Policies are **auditable** (reasoning traces captured per run)
- Policies are **human-authored** (agents can propose updates, never self-approve)
- **Default-to-page**: Any incident not matching a known policy pattern immediately escalates

### 2.4 Policy-as-Code (WORKFLOW.md Pattern)

From OpenAI Symphony's model (adapted by Atlassian):

```yaml
# WORKFLOW.md front matter
tracker:
  api_key: $LINEAR_API_KEY
workspace:
  root: $SYMPHONY_WORKSPACE_ROOT
hooks:
  after_create: |
    git clone --depth 1 "$SOURCE_REPO_URL" .
codex:
  command: "$CODEX_BIN app-server --model gpt-5.3-codex"
```

The markdown body contains the prompt template (policy expression) for the agent. Policy changes are PRs with commit history — rollback is a git revert.

### 2.5 Guardrails Table (What Agents Must NOT Do Autonomously)

| Failure Mode | Risk | Guardrail |
|-------------|------|-----------|
| AI misdiagnoses root cause, applies wrong fix | HIGH | All autonomous fixes staged until confidence threshold met or human approval |
| Policy gap — incident type not covered | HIGH | Default-to-page: immediate escalation to on-call. Silence is never default. |
| Remediation PR introduces new failure mode | MEDIUM | Full Validate-stage eval before merge (chaos brief + shadow execution) |
| Policy drift — policies become stale | MEDIUM | Policies versioned alongside Spec; governance rotation reviews on cadence |
| SRE loses codebase familiarity | MEDIUM | Deep-Dive Rotations maintain technical grounding for validating Reasoning Traces |

**Source**: [Agentic Operations in the New SDLC](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~71202027e1af5d30e94112880666ee62f9277a/pages/6885869718)

