## Stream 3: RFC-002 — AI SDLC Governance Framework (L0–L5 Autonomy Levels)

### 3.1 What It Is

RFC-002 is Atlassian's **composable governance framework** for assessing and scaling AI usage across the SDLC. It defines:
- **Readiness levels** (R0–R3): Team/repo preparedness to adopt AI
- **Maturity levels** (L0–L5): Demonstrated AI workflow quality justifying reduced human oversight
- **Risk taxonomy**: Scope × Criticality × Sensitivity × Reversibility
- **Progression gates**: Evidence-based advancement requirements

**Status**: Under review (due2026-05-29)
**Driver**: Rebecca Fitzhugh
**Source**: [RFC-002 - AI SDLC Governance Framework](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/AISDLC/pages/6849219616)

### 3.2 The L0–L5 Maturity Model

| Level | Name | AI Role | Human Decision Authority | Typical Pattern |
|-------|------|---------|-------------------------|-----------------|
| **L0** | No meaningful AI involvement | Absent or incidental | Humans perform all work and approvals | Manual workflow |
| **L1** | Assistive | Suggests or drafts | Humans review all outputs before use | Copilot-style support |
| **L2** | Structured assistance | Performs bounded tasks with workflow integration | Humans retain explicit approval on every action | Integrated recommendations with audit trail |
| **L3** | Conditional autonomy | Executes or adjudicates in low-risk cases | Humans oversee exceptions and sampled outcomes | Automation within risk and policy guardrails |
| **L4** | High autonomy | Handles most in-scope tasks autonomously | Humans govern policy, thresholds, and incident response | Autonomous operation with strong monitoring |
| **L5** | Near-full autonomy | Operates as primary decision-maker for eligible work | Humans provide governance, audits, and emergency intervention | Highly trusted closed-loop system |

**Decision authority progression**: human-only → human-approved → human-supervised → human-governed

### 3.3 Readiness Levels (Sets Ceiling on Maturity)

| Readiness Level | Name | Domain Maturity Ceiling |
|-----------------|------|------------------------|
| **R0** | Not Ready | L1 (assistive only) |
| **R1** | Low / Foundational | L2 (structured assistive) |
| **R2** | Medium / Enabled | L3 (conditional autonomy) |
| **R3** | High / Optimized | L4-L5 (subject to risk ceilings) |

### 3.4 Risk Taxonomy

| Risk Class | Characteristics | Default Autonomy Ceiling |
|-----------|-----------------|-------------------------|
| **Low** | Narrow scope, low criticality, easily reversible | L4-L5 |
| **Moderate** | Bounded scope, moderate criticality, reversible with effort | L3-L4 |
| **High** | Broader impact, significant security implications, limited reversibility | L2-L3 |
| **Restricted** | Highly sensitive, regulated, difficult to reverse | L0-L2 (unless explicit exception) |

**Core rule**: No domain may exceed the autonomy ceiling of its current risk class without documented exception, evidence, and approval.

### 3.5 Progression Gates

| Gate | Description | Example Evidence |
|------|-------------|-----------------|
| **Concordance** | Degree AI decisions align with human judgment | Blind comparison studies, reviewer agreement analysis |
| **Failure rate parity** | AI-assisted workflow must not create worse outcomes than human baseline | Defect escape rate, rollback rate, false positive/negative analysis |
| **Calibration** | Confidence signals must correlate with actual correctness | Reliability curves, precision-at-threshold metrics |
| **Coverage** | Evaluated on sufficiently representative task distribution | Sample distribution, scenario matrix, cohort coverage |
| **Feedback loops** | Mechanism to capture errors, overrides, drift signals | Override logging, incident taxonomy, retraining backlog |

### 3.6 Cross-Domain Dependencies

| Dependent Domain Claim | Minimum Dependency | Rationale |
|------------------------|-------------------|-----------|
| Code Review at L3 | Testing at L2 | Autonomous low-risk approval requires trustworthy automated testing |
| Deploy at L3 | Observability at L3 | Autonomous deployment requires strong detection/diagnosis capability |
| CI/CD orchestration at L4 | Policy enforcement at L3 + rollback at L3 | High-autonomy execution unsafe without hard controls |
| Incident response at L3 | Traceability at L3 + restricted action handling | Even bounded autonomy requires complete action visibility |

### 3.7 Baseline Capabilities (Non-Negotiable for Any Domain)

| Capability | Requirement |
|-----------|-------------|
| **Traceability** | Record model version, prompt/context, inputs, outputs, decisions, outcomes |
| **Evaluation infrastructure** | Repeatable offline/online evaluations with versioned datasets |
| **Policy enforcement** | Guardrails for restricted actions, data access, approval requirements |
| **Isolation and rollback** | Constrain blast radius and rapidly reverse AI-driven changes |
| **Observability** | Monitor behavior, latency, override rates, failure patterns, drift |
| **Accountability** | Named owners for domain operation, platform support, incident response |

### 3.8 Design Principles

| Principle | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Composable by domain | Maturity assessed independently per SDLC domain |
| Shared structure, flexible implementation | Common vocabulary for levels/risk/gates; concrete evidence varies |
| Risk-bounded autonomy | Decision authority capped by work risk profile |
| Evidence-based progression | Advancement requires observable evidence |
| No single aggregate score | Maturity profiles across domains, not blended numbers |

