## 6. Industry Best Practices & Frameworks

### 6.1 MLOps Level Classification (Google)

| Level | Description | Retraining Approach |
|---|---|---|
| Level 0 | Manual, notebook-driven | Ad hoc, when someone remembers |
| Level 1 | ML pipeline automation | Scheduled (e.g., weekly/monthly) |
| Level 2 | CI/CD pipeline automation | Event-triggered (drift detected → retrain) |

**Atlassian's current state**: Most teams are at Level 0-1. The drift-triggered retraining pattern represents Level 2 maturity.

### 6.2Retraining Strategy Options

| Strategy | Description | Best For |
|---|---|---|
| Scheduled | Retrain every N days | Stable domains, predictable drift |
| Event-triggered | Retrain on drift/performance signal | Dynamic domains, unpredictable drift |
| Stateless | Train from scratch on last N days | When concept changes completely |
| Stateful | Fine-tune on new data since last training | Gradual drift, preserve knowledge |

**Recommendation for skill**: Default to event-triggered + stateless for safety. Stateful (fine-tuning) as optimization only after proven stable.

### 6.3 Key Metrics for the Complete Loop

| Phase | Metric | Target |
|---|---|---|
| Detection | Time to Detection (MTTD) | < 1 day from drift onset |
| Trigger | Time from detection to retraining start | < 4 hours (with cooldown) |
| Training | Training duration | Model-dependent |
| Validation | Champion/challenger comparison time | < 2 hours |
| Deployment | Shadow evaluation period | 1-7 days |
| Deployment | Canary period per stage | 24h minimum |
| Rollback | Time to rollback execution | < 5 minutes |
| Recovery | Time to resolution after rollback | Investigation-dependent |

---

## 7. Key Patterns for Skill/Tool Encoding

### 7.1 Anti-Patterns to Encode as Guards

1. **Infinite retraining loop**: Drift → retrain → deploy → still drifts → retrain... (Guard: cooldown period, max retrain frequency, disable after rollback)
2. **Flaky detector rollbacks**: Misconfigured thresholds cause unnecessary rollbacks (Guard: validate thresholds against actual baseline before activation)
3. **Silent degradation**: Model degrades but no one notices (Guard: always have performance monitoring active, negative alerting for missing metrics)
4. **Over-retraining on noise**: Single spike triggers expensive retraining (Guard: require sustained signal, correlation across multiple features)
5. **Deployment without shadow**: Skipping shadow mode to save time (Guard: non-negotiable shadow period in deployment pipeline)

### 7.2 Configuration Schema for Drift-Triggered Retraining

```yaml
drift_retraining_config:
  detection:
    metrics:
      - type: ks_pvalue
        threshold: 0.05
        features: ["*"]  # all registered features
      - type: psi
        threshold: 0.2
        features: ["*"]
    correlation_rule: ">=3features drifting simultaneously"
    performance_metric:
      name: "ndcg@10"
      degradation_threshold: 0.02# 2pp drop
  trigger:
    cooldown_hours: 24
    max_retrains_per_week: 3
    require_performance_degradation: true  # don't retrain on drift alone
    
  validation:
    baseline_strategy: "champion_model"
    metrics_to_compare: ["ndcg@10", "precision@5", "recall@10"]
    pass_criteria: "candidate >= baseline on all metrics"
    
  deployment:
    shadow_period_days: 3
    canary_stages: [0.10, 0.50, 1.00]
    soak_hours_per_stage: 24
    
  rollback:
    critical_signals:
      - metric: "error_rate_5xx"
        threshold: 0.05
        window: "5m"
      - metric: "circuit_breaker_open"
        threshold: 1high_signals:
      - metric: "latency_p99_increase_pct"
        threshold: 50consecutive_breaches_for_rollback: 3
    post_rollback_cooldown_hours: 72
```

This configuration schema can be directly encoded in a SKILL.md file as the canonical template for drift-triggered retraining workflows.

