## 1. Signal Correlation — Drift Detection as Retraining Trigger

### 1.1 ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection (Atlassian Internal)

**What it is**: A platform-level drift detection solution that automatically computes drift metrics for all MLFS-registered features, detecting distributional shifts that indicate data quality issues or model performance degradation.

**How it works**:
- **Statistical tests applied**:
  - KS Test (Kolmogorov-Smirnov): D-statistic and p-value for continuous distributions
  - PSI (Population Stability Index): Magnitude of distributional shift via binned comparisons
  - Chi-squared test: For categorical features- Summary statistics: mean, stddev, min, max, skewness, kurtosis
- **Reference strategies**:
  - Sliding window (default28 days)
  - Fixed reference period (absolute dates)
  - User-supplied baseline (summary stats only in MVP)
- **Computation cadence**: Every ~5 minutes via scheduled drift calculator
- **Metric emission**: `feature_observability.drift.ks_d`, `feature_observability.drift.ks_pvalue`, `feature_observability.drift.psi`
- **Auto-selection**: Calculator automatically selects applicable test per feature type (KS for numeric, Chi-squared for categorical) without per-feature configuration

**Alert Configuration (SDK)**:
```python
AlertConfig(alert_namespace="my-team.features", alert_key="drift").alert_message("Drift detected in feature distributions.")
    .runbook("<runbook-url>")
    .alert_config(
        AlertMetricConfig(metric_namespace="feature_observability.drift.ks_pvalue").condition(AlertCondition.single(Condition(measure="expected_value", operator="<=", value=0.05)))
        .tag_filter("feature_name", "=", "daily_transaction_count")
        .tag_filter("feature_service", "=", "fraud_detection_v2")
    )
    .alert_config(
        AlertMetricConfig(metric_namespace="feature_observability.drift.ks_d")
        .condition(AlertCondition.single(Condition(measure="expected_value", operator=">=", value=0.1)))
    )
    .recipient(Recipient.slack(RecipientValue(id="#ml-alerts", type="channel")))
```

**How it applies to skill/tool**: The AI employee should be able to:
1. Configure drift detection thresholds per feature/model
2. Interpret drift signals (KS p-value≤ 0.05, PSI > threshold) as retraining triggers
3. Correlate multiple drift signals to decide if retraining is needed vs. a data pipeline issue
4. Distinguish between gradual drift (trend break) and sudden shift (distribution collapse)

**Source**: [ML Lens Platform Data Drift Detection Design](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6738511349/Design+ML+Lens+Platform+Data+Drift+Detection), [ML Lens Alerting Requirements](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5989280688/ML+Lens+Alerting+Requirements)

---

### 1.2 Alert Pattern Catalog for Drift Signal Correlation

**What it is**: A structured catalog of drift alert patterns that enable multi-signal correlation.

**How it works** — Pattern types:

| Pattern | Condition Examples | Use Cases |
|---|---|---|
| Distribution drift (continuous) | std >= 3 (z-score); abs(mean_current - mean_ref) > threshold | Model score drift; recall set score distribution shift |
| Categorical distribution shift | PSI > threshold; chi-squared p-value < alpha | Traffic mix shift; missing categories |
| Plateau/zeroing | std == 0 with mean == 0; >x% zeros/NaNs vs reference | Feature loader failure; truncation |
| Trend break (week-over-week) | relative change > threshold | Seasonality-aware detection; gradual drifts |
| Negative alerting (missing metrics) | no metric seen for SLA window; category not observed vs ref set | Pipeline stalls; silent feature retirement |

**Signal correlation logic for retraining decision**:
1. Single-feature drift → investigate (may be data issue)
2. Multiple correlated features drifting → likely retraining trigger
3. Model output drift + feature drift → strong retraining signal
4. Feature drift without model degradation → monitor (model may be robust)
5. Model degradation without feature drift → investigate model itself (concept drift)

**How it applies to skill/tool**: The skill should encode a decision tree:
- If KS p-value < 0.05 AND PSI > 0.2 for≥3 features → recommend retraining
- If model performance metric (NDCG/accuracy) drops > X% → escalate to immediate retraining
- If only1 feature drifts → investigate data pipeline first
- Human confirmation required before triggering retraining when signal is ambiguous

**Source**: [ML Lens Alerting Requirements](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/5989280688/ML+Lens+Alerting+Requirements), [Feature Drift Detection Architecture Proposal](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/KYC/pages/6866709029/Feature+Drift+Detection+Architecture+Proposal)

---

### 1.3 Automated Retraining via StreamHub Integration (ML Studio Orchestrator)

**What it is**: An event-driven architecture where ML Lens emits drift/quality metrics to StreamHub, consumed by ML Studio Orchestrator to trigger retraining workflows.

**How it works**:
1. ML Lens computes drift metrics on schedule (daily via Cronman or ~5min cadence)
2. Metrics/expectations published to StreamHub (event bus)
3. ML Studio Orchestrator subscribes to StreamHub events
4. Orchestrator evaluates user-configured retraining conditions
5. If conditions met → triggers ML Studio workflow for retraining
6. Retraining produces new model artifact registered in MLflow/ML Registry

**Configurable conditions** (user-defined):
- Data quality thresholds: null count > 20%, mean value shift > X std
- Drift metrics: KS p-value < alpha, PSI > threshold
- Performance metrics: accuracy drop > Y% (future extension)
- Composite conditions: AND/OR logic across multiple signals

**Work plan milestones**:
1. Orchestrator listens to events in StreamHub
2. Support model re-training conditions and validation of conditions
3. Announce availability of re-training using data quality

**Scope limitations (current)**:
- Automatic deployment NOT included (remains manual)
- Only data quality triggers in MVP; model performance triggers as future extension
- Human evaluation still required before deployment

**How it applies to skill/tool**: The skill should:
- Define the workflow: Drift detected → Signal evaluated → Retraining triggered → Baseline reproduced → Candidate validated → Human approval → Deploy
- Clearly separate autonomous steps (detection, retraining) from human-gated steps (deployment approval)
- Include cooldown periods to prevent over-retraining

**Source**: [RFC: Model Re-training using Data Quality and ML Lens](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/6013257858/RFC+Model+Re-training+using+Data+Quality+and+ML+Lens)

