# Research: Runbook Authoring Templates and Refresh Cadence

## Executive Summary

This research synthesizes findings from Atlassian's internal practices, industry frameworks, and real-world implementations on runbook authoring templates and refresh cadence management. The findings are organized to directly inform skill/tool specification for an AI employee that needs to author, maintain, and enforce freshness of operational runbooks in ML/AI platform contexts.

---

## Finding 1: Comprehensive Runbook Template Structure (Canonical Sections)

### What it is
A standardized runbook template with 17 sections covering the full lifecycle from metadata through procedure execution to revision history. Multiple templates exist within Atlassian, but they converge on a common skeleton.

### How it works
The **Comprehensive Runbook Template** (most complete version found) contains:

```
1. General Information (metadata table)
   - Data Product/Service, Owner Team, Point of Contact, Escalation
   - Last Revision Date, Status (Active/Deprecated/Draft)
2. Purpose
   - When to use, Audience, Expected Outcome
3. System Background
   - Architecture diagram, Dashboards, Code/Repo, Related docs
4. Pipeline Summary (if applicable)
   - DAG/Flow Name, Refresh Frequency, ETC, Data Availability
5. Severity, Priority & SLA
   - Business Period, Severity, Time to Respond, Time to Resolve
6. Impacted Consumers & Stakeholders
7. Impacted Dashboards (optional)
8. Preconditions & Prerequisites
   - Permissions, Tools/environment, Safety checks, Approvals
9. Step-by-Step Procedure
   - 9.1 Preparation (validate, confirm context)
   - 9.2 Execution (tabular: Step | Action | Command | Expected output)
   - 9.3 Post-Execution Validation
10. Rollback / Recovery
11. Issue Type and Support (escalation matrix)
12. Debugging Pipeline Failure (if applicable)
13. Known Errors (table: Alert/Error | Type | Reason | Resolution)
14. Troubleshooting
15. Customer Impact & Risks
16. References & Further Reading
17. FAQ
+ Revision History (Date | Author | Changes)
```

**Source**: [Comprehensive Runbook Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~712020bddf94c6ee68467cb02b0dc8080ec3b0/pages/6560528436/Comprehensive+Runbook+Template)

### How it applies
The AI skill should use this as the **maximal template** when generating runbooks. For ML/AI services, sections 3-5 are critical (system background including model architecture, SLO thresholds, latency budgets). The skill should be able to:
- Generate a full runbook from this template given service metadata
- Validate existing runbooks against this structure
- Identify missing sections and flag them for completion

---

## Finding 2: AI-Ready Incident/Alert Response Runbook Template

### What it is
A specialized template designed for machine-readability byIncidentAI and OpsSherpa, with structured metadata conventions and AI hint blocks.

### How it works
The template follows this structure:

```
Metadata Header:Status: DRAFT | UNDER REVIEW | ACTIVE
  Summary: <runbook summary>
  SME(s): <subject matter experts>
  Related runbooks: <links>
  Date last reviewed: <date>

Sections:
1. Assumptions- Required permissions/access to specific tools, micros panels, etc.
2. Initial Triage
   - Step 1: Gather information (regions, services affected)
   - Step 2: Assess impact
   - Step 3: Isolate the problem (decision matrix)
3. Mitigation
   - Procedure steps with expected outcomes
   - "If stuck → Escalation"
4. Root Cause Investigation
5. Escalation
   - Who, when, how
6. AI Hints (for AI consumers)
   - Thresholds: what counts as "high" vs "normal"
   - Naming conventions: how to identify environments/components- Signal vs noise: patterns that look bad but aren't
   - Known false positives: conditions to dismiss
```

**Alert linking requirements** forIncidentAI integration:
- Alert description must contain: `Runbook: <full_confluence_page_URL>`
- The `incident-ai` label is added to the runbook page AFTER alert link is configured

**Source**: [AI-Ready Incident/Alert Response Runbook Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PRODUCT/pages/6975464471/Template+AI-Ready+Incident+Alert+Response+Runbook), [Jira AI Incident/Alert Response Runbook Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/jai/pages/5435625651/Jira+AI+Incident+Alert+Response+Runbook+Template), [Forge Storage Runbook Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ECO/pages/6541250648/Forge+Storage+Runbook+Template)

### How it applies
For the AI employee skill, this template provides the **AI-parseable format**. Key skill behaviors:
- When authoring runbooks, always include the `AI Hints` section with thresholds, naming conventions, signal-vs-noise, and known false positives
- Ensure alert→runbook linking follows the exact format required by IncidentAI
- The "Assumptions" section should explicitly state required access/permissions so the AI employee knows what credentials it needs before execution

---

## Finding 3: Runbook-as-Code Pattern (Executable Runbooks)

### What it is
An emerging pattern to store runbooks in version-controlled code repositories with structured, programmatic definitions that support interactive execution, validation, and automated publishing.

### How it works
From the Trello Infrastructure RFC and Search Platform Experience OpsAI implementation:

**Repository Structure:**
```
repo/
├── docs/runbooks/           # Agentic runbooks (structured markdown)
│   ├── entry.md             # Index of all runbooks
│   ├── alert-name-1.md
│   └── alert-name-2.md
├── .rovodev/skills/         # AI agent skills
│   ├── ops-onboarding/
│   ├── alert-investigation/
│   └── runbook/# Runbook execution contract
├── signalfx/terraform/      # Dashboards, detectors, SLOs
└── splunk/                  # Log dashboards
```

**Key features:**
1. **Hierarchical Step Organization** — Sequential vs Exclusive (user-choice) sub-steps
2. **Runtime Validation** — Validates all steps have associated functions, unique names
3. **Interactive Execution** — User confirmation before each step
4. **Version Control** — PRs/reviews for runbook changes
5. **Auto-publish to Confluence** — Via Pipefluence on merge to master

**Apollo Runbook Template step types** (for executable runbooks):
```
AUTOMATED— executed without human intervention
HUMAN_GATE   — pauses and waits for human signal
VERIFY— automated check; asserts condition
DECISION     — branches based on input values or state
```

Each step has: Type | Description | Action | Success Criteria | On Failure

**Source**: [RFC - Runbooks as Code](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/~70121b8feae607d83459ea04a575c4f8923d4/pages/5761935001/RFC+-+Runbooks+as+Code), [OpsAI - Build AI native workflow](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CASE/pages/6678923067/OpsAI+-+Build+AI+native+workflow+for+operation+work+including+oncall+and+disturbed), [Apollo Runbook Template](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DMP/pages/6759157050/Apollo+Runbook+Template)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Author runbooks with explicit step types (AUTOMATED vs HUMAN_GATE) so the AI knows which steps it can execute autonomously vs. where it must pause for human confirmation
- Include success criteria and on-failure paths for every step — enabling autonomous decision-making during execution
- Store runbooks in version-controlled repos with CI validation
- Auto-sync to Confluence for human consumption

---

## Finding 4: Runbook Metadata Schema for Freshness Tracking

### What it is
A YAML metadata block embedded at the top of each runbook file that enables automated freshness scanning and review ticket generation.

### How it works
From the CSRE (Cloud SRE) quarterly runbook review process:

```yaml
---
title: "Runbook title"
status: live|deprecated|needs_work
type: alert|operation|howto
tags:
  - tag1
  - tag2
notes: "Optional notes"
last_reviewed_at: "YYYY-MM-DD"
---
```

**Freshness rule:** If `last_reviewed_at` is older than **6 months**, the runbook is flagged for review in the next cycle.

**Complementary metadata fields** (from other templates):
```
Owner: <team or individual>
Revision Date: <last update date>
Next Review Date: <scheduled next review>
Version: <semantic version>
Status: DRAFT | UNDER REVIEW | ACTIVE
FedRamp Status: FULLY FUNCTIONAL | CONTAINS MODIFICATIONS | COMMERCIAL ONLY
```

**Freshness footer pattern** (from responsible-ai-api optimization work):
- A visible footer on the runbook page showing last-reviewed date
- Tony Chen's own work item [OPP-202605-09] explicitly calls out "primary on-call runbook content matches current IaC and carries a freshness footer"

**Source**: [Quarterly CSRE Runbook Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CSRE/pages/2744796152/Quarterly+CSRE+Runbook+Review), [responsible-ai-api Optimization](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-262)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Always include metadata headers in generated runbooks
- Implement a freshness-checking workflow that scans metadata and flags stale runbooks
- Generate a freshness footer at the bottom of every runbook page
- The threshold for "stale" should be configurable (default: 6 months, with option for3 months for critical services)

---

## Finding 5: Automated Refresh Cadence and Review Process

### What it is
A fully automated quarterly runbook review process with CLI tooling, Jira ticket generation, and scheduled Bitbucket pipelines.

### How it works
**CSRE Implementation:**

1. **Quarterly cadence** triggered by a Bitbucket pipeline cron schedule:
   ```
   cron_pattern: "0 0 0 1 1,4,7,10 ? *"  # 1st of Jan, Apr, Jul, Oct
   ```

2. **CLI tool** (`./scripts/docs`) performs two tasks:
   - `validate` — Scans runbooks for metadata format errors
   - `review` — Identifies outdated runbooks and creates Jira tickets

3. **Distribution** — Each team member is assigned ~2 review tickets per quarter

4. **Cleanup** — Unresolved tickets are deleted at the end of the cycle

**Alternative cadence patterns found:**

| Cadence | Trigger | Use Case |
|---------|---------|----------|
| Quarterly | Automated cron pipeline | Standard operational runbooks |
| Every 6 months | After any P1/P2 incident | Service-specific runbooks |
| Bi-annual | Calendar-based | Incident response plans |
| Event-driven | Post-incident, post-deploy | When content is known stale |
| Weekly | During critical phases | Migration/cutover runbooks |

**Governance table pattern** (from Turbopuffer):
```
| Artifact | Initial Owner | Next Review | Cadence |
|----------|--------------|-------------|---------|
| Runbook Index | Program Owner | 2026-2-15 | Quarterly |
| Incident Plan | IC | 2026-3-15 | Bi-annual |
```

**Source**: [Quarterly CSRE Runbook Review](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/CSRE/pages/2744796152/Quarterly+CSRE+Runbook+Review), [Turbopuffer General Runbooks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DMP/pages/5608192939/Turbopuffer+General+Runbooks), [J2 Daily Analytics & Event Publishing - Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/dhp1/pages/6674031323/J2+Daily+Analytics+Event+Publishing+-+Runbook)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should implement:
1. **Automated staleness scanning** — Run on a configurable schedule (quarterly default)
2. **Jira ticket creation** for each stale runbook with: title, link to runbook, assigned reviewer
3. **Freshness validation** as a CI gate — fail pipelines if a runbook linked to an active alert is stale
4. **Event-driven refresh triggers** — After a P1/P2 incident that used the runbook, auto-flag for review
5. **The governance table** as a required artifact in every runbook index page

---

## Finding 6: Engineering Maturity Model for Runbooks (5-Level Scoring Rubric)

### What it is
A standardized maturity assessment framework used across Atlassian Engineering to score team runbook practices, from absent to fully automated.

### How it works
**Scoring Rubric:**

| Level | Criteria |
|-------|----------|
| **Absent** | No runbooks; ad-hoc/undocumented/intuition-based troubleshooting |
| **Reactive** | Ad-hoc docs, no structure, inconsistent, scattered, outdated, wrong detail level |
| **Proactive** (minimum target) | Well-structured, follows predefined template; easy to find (centralized inventory, labels, go/link); easy to read; regularly tested and updated |
| **Strategic** | Official audit date listed (< 6 months old); common/recurring incidents automated, replacing manual runbooks |
| **Visionary** | Full automation — moving away from manual runbooks to fully automated runbook execution |

**Assessment process:**
- Teams fill out a "Runbooks Assessment Card" page
- A champion/approver reviews and assigns the level
- Teams below Proactive must create an action plan with steps and deadlines

**Source**: [JFP Devprod Runbooks Assessment Card](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/JFP/pages/6391733568/JFP+Devprod+Runbooks+Assessment+Card), [Jira Frontier - Reliability Practices - Writing Effective Runbooks](https://home.atlassian.com/o/2346a038-3c8c-498b-a79b-e7847859868d/s/a436116f-02ce-4520-8fbb-7301462a1674/project/ATLAS-95400)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Implement a maturity assessment capability that scores existing runbooks against this rubric
- For "Strategic" level: enforce the< 6 months audit date constraint
- For "Visionary" level: identify runbook steps that can be fully automated and flag them for code conversion
- Target at minimum "Proactive" for all generated runbooks (structured, template-based, labeled, discoverable)

---

## Finding 7: Runbook Quality Gates

### What it is
A set of quality criteria that runbooks must pass before being considered operational.

### How it works
From the Turbopuffer runbook governance framework:

1. **Clarity**: Purpose, scope, and signals are unambiguous; jargon is defined
2. **Actionability**: Steps are verifiable, time-bounded, and reversible where possible
3. **Maintainability**: Owners, review cadence, and dependencies are declared; avoid drift with automation hooks

From the Runbook Guidelines (Production Engineering):

**What makes a good runbook:**
> Efficiency — a step-by-step guide that is quick and intuitive to follow for anyone meeting the operational requirements

- **Quick** — Designed for execution speed
- **Straightforward** — Anyone with the right operational skills can follow it without extra implicit knowledge
- **Intuitive** — The format is self-guided and self-explanatory

**What a runbook is NOT:**
- Not a blog post (no magazine-style writing)
- Not something you create and forget (requires ongoing KTLO maintenance)
- Not a substitute for automation (aim to automate steps whenever possible)

**Validation checklist** (from Bitbucket SRE Database Alert Audit):
1. All paging alerts are actionable, unique, and relevant (no duplicates)
2. All alerts have a reviewed runbook attached
3. Alert urgencies and thresholds are reviewed
4. Continuous review process (TechOps) established with feedback loop
5. A single pane of glass for product health across all services

**Source**: [Turbopuffer General Runbooks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/DMP/pages/5608192939/Turbopuffer+General+Runbooks), [Runbook Guidelines](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/PRE/pages/1118680477/Runbook+Guidelines), [Bitbucket SRE Database Alert, Runbook, and Observability Audit](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/SRE-622)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should validate every runbook it generates against these quality gates before publishing:
- Is every step verifiable (has success criteria)?
- Is every step time-bounded (has a timeout or expected duration)?
- Is every step reversible (has a rollback or "on failure" path)?
- Are owners and review cadence declared?
- Is jargon defined inline or linked?
- Are dashboards/monitoring links included and valid?

---

## Finding 8: Runbook Types and When to Use Each

### What it is
A taxonomy of runbook types that determines which template structure to use.

### How it works
From multiple internal sources, runbooks fall into distinct categories:

| Type | Description | Template Focus |
|------|-------------|---------------|
| **Alert/Alarm Runbook** | Linked to a specific alert/detector; activated when alert fires | Triage → Mitigation → Root Cause → Escalation |
| **Task/Operation Runbook** | Procedures for carrying out a maintenance/operational task | Prerequisites → Steps → Validation → Rollback |
| **How-To Runbook** | Knowledge article for completing a specific procedure | Context → Steps → Verification → References |
| **Incident Response Playbook** | Cross-team coordination during major incidents | Detection → Communication → Mitigation → PIR |

**Naming convention** (from Marathon framework):
```
Type of Runbook with target user | The specific runbook focus | Further breakdown
```
Examples:
- `Alert Runbook | High CPU on inference endpoint | SageMaker`
- `Task Runbook | Model promotion to production | Tarot Registry`

**Source**: [Task: How to write runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/ADR/pages/1859049348/Task+How+to+write+runbook), [Guide to creating Runbooks & Playbooks in Marathon](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MCENTRAL/pages/6446022294/Guide+to+creating+Runbooks+Playbooks+in+Marathon)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Classify the runbook type based on context (is this for an alert? a task? an incident?)
- Select the appropriate template variant based on type
- Apply the naming convention consistently
- Tag/label accordingly for discoverability

---

## Finding 9: Freshness Footer Implementation Pattern

### What it is
A visible maintenance metadata block placed at the bottom (or top) of runbooks to communicate freshness state to humans and AI consumers.

### How it works
**Standard footer format** (from multiple implementations):

```markdown
## Runbook Maintenance
| Field| Value                |
|--------------------|--------------------------|
| Review frequency   | Every 6 months or after any P1/P2 incident |
| Last reviewed      | 2026-03-26|
| Reviewed by        | @engineer_name           |
| Next review due    | 2026-10-10               |
```

**Alternative header format** (from older templates):
```
| Status| IN USE|
| Owner           | Team Name             |
| Related RBs     | [links]               |
| Last Reviewed   | 2025-12-06|
| Next Review     | 2026-06-06            |
```

**Staleness thresholds in practice:**
- responsible-ai-api runbook: flagged as ~470 days stale → clearly critical
- Standard threshold: 6 months (180 days)
- Critical services: 3 months (90 days)
- Post-incident trigger: immediate review regardless of age

**Source**: [J6 Security Insights - Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/dhp1/pages/6679359626/J6+Security+Insights+-+Runbook), [responsible-ai-api Optimization](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-262), [J2 Daily Analytics & Event Publishing - Runbook](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/dhp1/pages/6674031323/J2+Daily+Analytics+Event+Publishing+-+Runbook)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Always generate a freshness footer on every runbook it creates
- Calculate and populate "Next review due" based on configurable cadence
- When updating a runbook, always update the footer fields
- Flag runbooks past their review date with a visual warning
- After any incident that references the runbook, reset the "Next review due" to current_date + 14 days

---

## Finding 10: ML/AI-Specific Runbook Considerations

### What it is
Domain-specific extensions to standard runbook templates for ML/AI services including model inference endpoints, training pipelines, and data pipelines.

### How it works
Based on the responsible-ai-api work and ML Platform Runbooks Structure:

**Additional sections for ML/AI runbooks:**
- **Model Information**: Model version, framework, endpoint type (real-time/batch)
- **SLO Configuration**: Per-endpoint latency targets, error budgets, burn rates
- **Feature Flags**: Kill switches for model features, rollback gates
- **Capacity & Scaling**: GPU autoscaling rules, ASG floor values, AZ rotation
- **Dependency Health**: Upstream model servers (Triton, SageMaker), sidecars (OTel)
- **Tenant Impact**: Per-tenant rate limits, blast radius metrics

**Operational primer concept** (from ML Platform Runbooks Structure):
> A common challenge for new team members going on-call for the first time is a lack of general familiarity with services. It's often not useful to land on a runbook linked by an alert unless you have at least a high-level understanding of the service it relates to.

This suggests a two-level structure:
1. **Operational Primer** — High-level service architecture, purpose, common operations
2. **Alert-specific Runbooks** — Linked from specific detectors, focused on one failure mode

**Source**: [ML Platform Runbooks Structure](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/2881827475/ML+Platform+Runbooks+Structure), [responsible-ai-api Optimization](https://hello.jira.atlassian.cloud/browse/AI-262)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Extend the standard template with ML-specific sections (model info, SLO, capacity)
- Generate an operational primer for each service it manages
- Ensure per-endpoint SLO information is encoded both in IaC AND in the runbook
- Include feature flag references with explicit instructions on how to use kill switches
- Document tenant isolation and blast radius for multi-tenant ML services

