## Finding 11: Runbook Discoverability and Organization

### What it is
Best practices for organizing and discovering runbooks so they can be found quickly during incidents.

### How it works
**Organization requirements** (from maturity model "Proactive" level):
- Located in a team's centralized "runbooks inventory"
- Tagged with Confluence labels
- Easily discoverable with a go/link (e.g., `go/team-runbooks`)
- Linked from alerts (alert description contains runbook URL)

**ML Platform structure** (recommended):
```
Team Space/
├── Runbooks/
│   ├── Index (master list with links)
│   ├── Operational Primers/
│   │   ├── Service A Overview
│   │   └── Service B Overview
│   ├── Alert Runbooks/
│   │   ├── Alert: High Latency on Endpoint X
│   │   └── Alert: Model Drift Detected
│   └── Task Runbooks/
│       ├── Task: Model Promotion
│       └── Task: Feature Store Backfill
├── On-Call Info/
│   ├── Onboarding
│   └── Alert Routing
└── Incident History/
```

**Source**: [ML Platform Runbooks Structure](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/MLP/pages/2881827475/ML+Platform+Runbooks+Structure), [TWGRFC-015Improving On-Call Runbooks](https://hello.atlassian.net/wiki/spaces/TEAMGRAPH/pages/6303210478/TWGRFC-015+Improving+On+Call+Runbooks)

### How it applies
The AI employee skill should:
- Create and maintain a runbook index page as a single entry point
- Apply consistent Confluence labels to all generated runbooks
- Include cross-references ("Related runbooks" and "Alternatives" sections)
- Ensure every alert/detector has a runbook URL linked in its configuration

